// Practice quiz: Calling the Spring Boot API from FastAPI: service URL, token and timeouts
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. First draft, not reviewed. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: python/fastapi-services/calling-the-spring-boot-api-from-fastapi-service-url-token-and-timeouts

::o1 settings::Why load the Spring Boot base URL and token through a settings class? {
=They come from the environment, so code holds no secret and each deployment can differ #Correct. Settings read environment variables and validate them at startup.
~httpx refuses hard-coded URLs #Not quite. httpx accepts any URL; this is about configuration and secrets.
~It makes the call faster #Not quite. It does not affect speed.
~It encrypts the token on the wire #Not quite. TLS protects the token in transit, not the settings class.
}

::o1 bearer::How is a bearer token normally sent? {
=In the Authorization header, as Bearer followed by the token #Correct. This is the RFC 6750 header form.
~In the URL path #Not quite. URLs often end up in logs, so avoid that.
~As a query parameter named bearer #Not quite. The header form is the recommended one, and query strings leak into logs.
~In the JSON response #Not quite. A request credential travels in the request.
}

::o2 default timeout::What is the httpx default when no timeout is passed? {
=A 5-second timeout #Correct. Unlike many clients, httpx applies a timeout by default.
~No timeout at all #Not quite. That needs an explicit timeout\=None.
~60 seconds #Not quite. The documented default is 5 seconds.
~It copies the upstream setting #Not quite. The client sets its own timeout.
}

::o2 order::Why catch httpx.TimeoutException before httpx.TransportError? {
=TimeoutException is a subclass of TransportError, and the first matching except wins #Correct. Reversing the order means the timeout branch never runs.
~Python requires alphabetical order #Not quite. It only checks clauses top to bottom.
~TransportError cannot be caught #Not quite. It can be caught.
~TimeoutException only happens once #Not quite. Frequency is not the reason.
}

::o3 mock::What does httpx.MockTransport let a handler do? {
=Receive the request and return a fixed response or raise an error #Correct. This tests your client code with no network.
~Start a Spring Boot service #Not quite. No service is started.
~Record real traffic automatically #Not quite. It replaces the network; it records nothing by itself.
~Check that the real token is valid #Not quite. It cannot check that without the real service.
}
